  <question type="essay">
    <name><text>E12 - Proof by contraposition (guided)</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Proof — Contraposition]</strong> Let \( n \in \mathbb{Z} \). Prove that <em>if \( n + 1 \) is even then \( n \) is odd</em>, using contraposition.</p>
<p><em>Hint:</em> the contrapositive is "if \(n\) is even then \(n + 1\) is odd". Start by assuming \(n\) is even, i.e. \(n = 2k\).</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Model proof.</strong> The contrapositive is: "if \(n\) is even then \(n + 1\) is odd." Assume \(n\) is even, so \(n = 2k\) for some integer \(k\). Then \(n + 1 = 2k + 1\), which is odd. So the contrapositive holds, and therefore the original statement "if \(n+1\) is even then \(n\) is odd" is true. \( \blacksquare \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
